<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuomintang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chinese Nationalist Party, KMT</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kuomintang, or Chinese Nationalist Party, is a political party founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and his allies in 1912, growing out of the revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> it reunited much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a> and ruled the Republic of China from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> from 1928. Defeated by the Communists in the civil war, it retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1949, governed there under martial law until 1987 and remains a major party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuomintang.jpg" alt="Kuomintang" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see above · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Kuomintang.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kuomintang traces its roots to the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, a proponent of Chinese nationalism and democracy. On 20 August 1905 Sun joined other anti-monarchist societies in Tokyo to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>, a group committed to overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and establishing a republic. The party's charter was adopted on 28 January 1924. Its guiding ideology was Sun's Three Principles of the People, namely Chinese nationalism, democracy and the people's economic livelihood, and it was organised on the Leninist principle of democratic centralism and supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>When Sun died in 1925, leadership passed formally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>, the left-wing leader, and Hu Hanmin, the right-wing leader, but real power lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, who controlled the military as superintendent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1926 to 1928 the party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a> against regional warlords, which led to the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a>. It had at first allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in the First United Front, but on 12 April 1927 Chiang violently purged the communists in what became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> massacre, which started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>.</p><p>The Nationalist government led five campaigns against the Chinese Soviet Republic and forced the communists into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> brought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a>. The party sought to build a one-party ideological state, called Dang Guo, and included the Blue Shirts Society, an ultranationalist paramilitary group modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s blackshirts.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s defeat in 1945, the KMT general Chen Yi accepted the Japanese surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> on 25 October. On 28 February 1947 the party put down an uprising there, and the White Terror began to purge suspected communist spies. In December 1949 the party retreated to Taiwan. It occupied assets formerly owned by the Japanese, and it oversaw rapid economic development, but lost the country's seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and diplomatic recognition by many states in the 1970s.</p><p>In the late 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-ching-kuo">Chiang Ching-kuo</a> lifted martial law and the ban on opposition parties. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-teng-hui">Lee Teng-hui</a> continued democratic reforms and was reelected in 1996 in the first direct presidential election. The 2000 presidential election ended 72 years of KMT presidencies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party's constitution designated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> as party president, a title kept in his memory, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> as Permanent President. The party was headed by a director-general from 1927 to 1975 and has had a chairman since 1975. It is run by a National Congress and Central Committee, and it has been divided into factions, among them the Whampoa Clique, the CC Clique and the Blue Shirt Society. Its support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> is concentrated in the urban north and ranges across social groups, largely determined by age.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuomintang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 1950s Chinese Nationalist (<b>Kuomintang</b>) troops who had fled into Shan State added another war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After its alliance with the <b>Kuomintang</b> collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in Jiangxi in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the Long March of 1934–35 to northern Shaanxi, where Yan’an became its base, during which Mao…</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the elections of 1912–13 Sun’s new <b>Kuomintang</b>, the Nationalist Party, won the largest share of seats, but its parliamentary leader Song Jiaoren was assassinated in March 1913.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kuomintang</b> members such as Luo Jialun, Shao Lizi and Duan Xipeng also took part, though the party’s influence on the movement was minimal.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Many leaders of the <b>Kuomintang</b>, including Liao Zhongkai, Hu Hanmin and Dai Jitao, took part in the movement, and played a major role in restructuring the party along Soviet lines in 1922–1924.</blockquote><a href="/movement/new-culture-movement">New Culture Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In China the <b>Kuomintang</b> proclaimed in 1928 a period of political tutelage under its sole rule.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
